/// @brief Shrinks an array by a given amount of elements
///
/// @comptime `T` The element type of the array to shrink
/// @param `arr` The array to shrink
/// @param `by` The number of elements to shrink the array by
///
/// @note `by` is clamped to `arr.len` so passing values of `by` which are larger than `arr.len`
///       is well-defined
/// @note Frees all elements from the end of the array which were removed
def shrink[type T](mut T[] arr, u64 by);

/// @brief Resizes the given array to the given size
///
/// @comptime `T` The element type of the array to resize
/// @param `arr` THe array to resize
/// @param `size` The size the array will have with after resizing
///
/// @note Since it is not known whether the array will grow or shrink at compile-time this function
///       requires that `T` is default-initializable.
///       If `T` is not default-initializable calling this function will result in compile errors.
def resize[type T](mut T[] arr, u64 size);

/// @brief Resizes the given array to the given size
///
/// @comptime `T` The element type of the array to resize
/// @param `arr` The array to resize
/// @param `size` The size the array will have after resizing
/// @param `default` The default-value which will be deep-cloned if the array needs to grow
def resize[type T](mut T[] arr, u64 size, T default);

/// @brief Inserts a given value at the given index into the given array
///
/// @comptime `T` The element type of the array to insert a value in
/// @param `arr` The array to insert the value in
/// @param `value` The value to insert into the array
/// @param `i` The index at which to insert the value at
///
/// @note The index `i` is clamped to `arr.len`. So if `i >= arr.len` is this
///       function will act like `append`.
/// @note General assignment-rules apply for the passed-in `value`, so it will
///       be deep-cloned if it is complex or just copied if non-complex.
def insert[type T](mut T[] arr, T value, u64 i);

/// @brief Removes a single element from the given array and returns it
///
/// @comptime `T` The element type of the array to remove a value from
/// @param `arr` The array to remove the value from
/// @param `i` The index at which to remove the value at
/// @return `T?` The removed value, `none` if `i` was out of the bounds of the array
def remove[type T](mut T[] arr, u64 i) -> T?;

/// @brief Merges the `src` array into the `arr` array, starting at index `i`
///
/// @comptime `T` The element type of the arrays
/// @param `arr` The target array in which the `src` array will be merged into
/// @param `src` The source array being merged into the `arr` array
/// @param `i` The index at which to merge the arrays
///
/// @note `src` will be empty after this call. All elements which were previously at `src` will end
///       up at `arr[i..(i + src.len)]`. Ownership of all elements within `src` is moved to `arr`.
/// @note This function does not clone any value, it essentially is just a realloc + up to two
///       memmoves under the hood.
def merge[type T](mut T[] arr, mut T[] src, u64 i);
